message.content:
{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2198-03-05 22:21:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I'm Dr. Lee. I’ll be taking care of you during your stay. Can you tell me what brought you to the hospital tonight?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2198-03-05 22:22:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Hi, Doctor. I’ve been vomiting nonstop since yesterday morning. I can’t keep anything down—water, food, nothing."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2198-03-05 22:23:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. Any abdominal pain, fever, or diarrhea?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2198-03-05 22:23:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No, no pain or fever. Just this awful nausea and vomiting. I had a really stressful day yesterday, and it started right after."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2198-03-05 22:24:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay. Given your history of gastroparesis and Type 1 diabetes, this could be another flare-up. We’ll keep you NPO for now and give IV fluids."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2198-03-05 22:25:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That makes sense. Last time, they thought it was gastroparesis too, but the tests were normal."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2198-03-05 22:26:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, I saw your records. You had a normal gastric emptying study recently. But since you’re having the same symptoms, we’ll treat it supportively for now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2198-03-05 22:26:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will I be able to eat soon?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2198-03-05 22:27:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not yet. We’ll wait until your nausea improves. We’re giving you Zofran, Compazine, and Ativan through your IV to help control it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2198-03-05 22:28:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, thanks. My blood sugar’s probably high too—I couldn’t take my insulin."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2198-03-05 22:28:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, your glucose was 312 on arrival. We gave you some insulin and will adjust your regimen. The diabetes team will help fine-tune it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2198-03-05 22:29:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve been worried about that. My sugars have been harder to control lately."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2198-03-05 22:30:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll monitor closely. Also, your blood pressure is quite high—172 over 112. That’s likely from the stress and not taking your meds."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2198-03-05 22:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "It always spikes in the hospital. I usually take lisinopril."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2198-03-05 22:31:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re giving IV hydralazine to bring it down safely. Once you can take pills, we’ll restart your lisinopril and may add another medication."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2198-03-06 08:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. How are you feeling? Any nausea?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2198-03-06 08:16:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Still nauseous, but maybe a little less. I haven’t vomited since last night."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2198-03-06 08:17:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s progress. We’ll keep the anti-nausea meds going. Your labs show ketones in your urine, so your sugars are still a bit high."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2198-03-06 08:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is that dangerous?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2198-03-06 08:18:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s a sign your body’s using fat for energy because it can’t use glucose well. We’re adjusting your insulin to fix that. Your Lantus dose will go up from 28 to 31 units at bedtime."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2198-03-06 08:19:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I can do that. Will I need to change my sliding scale too?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2198-03-06 08:20:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, the diabetes team is updating it to be more aggressive. We’ll give you the new instructions at discharge."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2198-03-06 14:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re going to do a CT scan of your head. Since you can’t have an MRI because of your gastric pacer, this will help rule out any brain-related causes for the nausea."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2198-03-06 14:31:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, right—I forgot about that. The MRI was planned last time, but my doctor said the pacer makes it unsafe."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2198-03-06 14:32:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. The CT with contrast will be a good alternative. It’s quick and should give us the answers we need."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2198-03-07 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good news—the CT scan showed no masses, bleeding, or stroke. Just a small old lacune, unchanged from before. So nothing in your brain is causing the nausea."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2198-03-07 09:01:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a relief. So it’s probably my gastroparesis again?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2198-03-07 09:02:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Most likely. Even though your last motility test was normal, symptoms can still flare. We’ll keep treating it as such."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2198-03-07 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Can I try sipping water today?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2198-03-07 09:03:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, let’s try small sips. If you keep it down for a few hours, we’ll advance to ice chips, then clear liquids."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2198-03-07 15:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve done great—no vomiting, and you tolerated the clear liquids. We’re going to try a soft diet this evening."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2198-03-07 15:01:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s great! I’m really hungry."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2198-03-07 15:02:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Just take it slow. We’ll still keep the anti-nausea meds on board for now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2198-03-08 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Morning! You made it through the night without vomiting. Your blood sugar is also improving—staying in the 150s with the new insulin dose."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2198-03-08 08:01:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a big improvement. What about my blood pressure?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2198-03-08 08:02:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Still a bit high, so we’ve added oral metoprolol twice daily. We’re also increasing your lisinopril to 20 mg daily."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2198-03-08 08:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve never taken metoprolol before. Any side effects I should watch for?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2198-03-08 08:03:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It can lower your heart rate and blood pressure. We’ll monitor it. Some people feel tired at first, but it usually settles."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2198-03-08 14:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re doing so well—we’re planning to discharge you tomorrow if this continues."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2198-03-08 14:01:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s great! What meds will I be going home with?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2198-03-08 14:02:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll continue your usual meds. Lantus is now 31 units at night. Lisinopril is 20 mg daily, and you’ll start metoprolol 50 mg twice a day."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2198-03-08 14:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And for nausea?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2198-03-08 14:03:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll have Zofran and Compazine tablets as needed. Plus, a new Compazine suppository—use it if pills don’t help."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2198-03-09 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Last night went well—no nausea, good sleep. We’ll aim for discharge this afternoon."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2198-03-09 09:01:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Should I see my endocrinologist soon?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2198-03-09 09:02:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, follow up with your PCP and endocrinologist within a week. They’ll review your insulin changes and monitor your blood pressure."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2198-03-09 14:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "All set—you’re cleared to go home. Here’s your discharge summary and prescriptions."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2198-03-09 14:01:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you so much. I feel so much better."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2198-03-09 14:02:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Remember: take the new meds as directed, advance your diet slowly, and call if nausea returns or your sugars go out of control."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2198-03-09 14:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. And I’ll schedule those follow-ups today."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 51,
      "time": "2198-03-10 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Just a quick final check before you leave. How are you feeling?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 52,
      "time": "2198-03-10 08:01:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Really good. No nausea, ate breakfast without any issues."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 53,
      "time": "2198-03-10 08:02:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Perfect. You’ve made excellent progress. Stay hydrated, keep monitoring your sugars, and don’t hesitate to reach out."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 54,
      "time": "2198-03-10 08:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thanks again for everything."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 55,
      "time": "2198-03-10 08:03:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Take care. We’ll see you in follow-up."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2198-03-05 22:21:00",
    "admission_end": "2198-03-10 14:18:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A 30-year-old woman with Type 1 diabetes and gastroparesis was admitted for recurrent intractable nausea and vomiting, unable to tolerate oral intake. She was managed with NPO status, IV fluids, and antiemetics including ondansetron, prochlorperazine, and lorazepam. Her blood glucose was elevated with ketonuria, prompting an increase in her Lantus dose from 28 to 31 units and adjustment of her sliding scale insulin. Hypertension, likely exacerbated by acute illness, was treated with IV hydralazine and transitioned to oral lisinopril 20 mg daily and metoprolol 50 mg twice daily. A CT head with contrast showed no central cause for her symptoms. Her nausea resolved, diet was advanced successfully, and she remained stable. She was discharged home with medication changes and instructions to follow up with her primary care provider and endocrinologist.",
    "problems": [
      "Diabetes with neurological manifestations, type I [juvenile type], uncontrolled",
      "Acidosis",
      "Gastroparesis",
      "Unspecified essential hypertension",
      "Esophageal reflux",
      "Acetonuria",
      "Persistent vomiting"
    ]
  }
}
